<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-subrings-activity">

	<title>Module 8: Subrings, Extensions, Direct Sums</title>
	<introduction><p><alert>Standing on Shoulders of Giants</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>

	<paragraphs>
		<title>What Properties are Inherited?</title>
		
    <p>
  Here are the properties of a ring.
  Say <m>R</m> is a ring.
  For each, do you think the property holds for any subset of <m>R</m>, or just
  some subsets of <m>R</m>?
	</p>
	
	<exercise>
      <p>
        The set <m>R</m> is closed under addition.
      </p>
      <sidebyside>
      <p>
        All subsets of <m>R</m> have this property
      </p>
      <p>
        Some subsets of <m>R</m> have this property.
      </p>
      </sidebyside>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        The set <m>R</m> is closed under multiplication.
      </p>
      <sidebyside>
      <p>
        All subsets of <m>R</m> have this property
      </p>
      <p>
        Some subsets of <m>R</m> have this property.
      </p>
      </sidebyside>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Addition is associative.
      </p>
      <sidebyside>
      <p>
        All subsets of <m>R</m> have this property
      </p>
      <p>
        Some subsets of <m>R</m> have this property.
      </p>
      </sidebyside>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Addition is commutative.
      </p>
      <sidebyside>
      <p>
        All subsets of <m>R</m> have this property
      </p>
      <p>
        Some subsets of <m>R</m> have this property.
      </p>
      </sidebyside>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        The set <m>R</m> contains an additive identity.
      </p>
      <sidebyside>
      <p>
        All subsets of <m>R</m> have this property
      </p>
      <p>
        Some subsets of <m>R</m> have this property.
      </p>
      </sidebyside>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Every element of <m>R</m> has an additive inverse within <m>R</m>.
      </p>
      <sidebyside>
      <p>
        All subsets of <m>R</m> have this property
      </p>
      <p>
        Some subsets of <m>R</m> have this property.
      </p>
      </sidebyside>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Multiplication is associative.
      </p>
      <sidebyside>
      <p>
        All subsets of <m>R</m> have this property
      </p>
      <p>
        Some subsets of <m>R</m> have this property.
      </p>
      </sidebyside>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Multiplication distributes over addition.
      </p>
      <sidebyside>
      <p>
        All subsets of <m>R</m> have this property
      </p>
      <p>
        Some subsets of <m>R</m> have this property.
      </p>
      </sidebyside>
    </exercise>

    </paragraphs>
    </page>


	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Subrings</title>
    
    	<definition>
			<p>
    Let <m>R</m> be a ring, and let <m>S</m> be a subset of <m>R</m>.
    Then <m>S</m> is said to be a <term>subring</term> of <m>R</m> provided
    that <m>S</m> itself is a ring with the operations of addition and
    multiplication defined the same as in <m>R</m>.
			</p>
		</definition>
		
		<p>
    As seen on the previous page - some properties of a ring are <q>inherited</q>
    for every subset of a ring <m>R</m>.
    This leads us to the subring test.
		</p>
		
		<theorem>
		<statement>
			Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>S</m> be a subset of <m>R</m>. Then <m>S</m> is a subring of <m>R</m> if
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>S</m> is closed under addition
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                <m>S</m> is closed under multiplication
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                <m>S</m> contains <m>0_{R}</m>
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                <m>S</m> is closed under additive inverses
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
		</statement>
		</theorem>
		
		<p>
    The Subring Test <trademark/> simplifies this even further, by combining
    closure under addition and closure under additive inverses into closure
    under subtraction.
    Also, if <m>S</m> is nonempty and closed under subtraction, then <m>S</m>
    contains <m>0_{R}</m>.
		</p>
		
		<theorem>
		<title>The Subring Test</title>
		<statement>
			Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>S</m> be a subset of <m>R</m>. Then <m>S</m> is a subring of <m>R</m> if and only if
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>S</m> is nonempty
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                <m>S</m> is closed under subtraction
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                <m>S</m> is closed under multiplication
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
		</statement>
		</theorem>
    
    
    <p>Let's work with subrings. Prove or disprove using the subring test:</p>
    
    <exercise workspace="1in">
		<p>The set <m>\mathbb{E}</m> of even integers is a subring of <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>.</p>
    </exercise>
    </paragraphs>

	</page>

	<page>
		<exercise workspace="1in">
		<p>The set
    		<md>
      		  K = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b \\ c &amp; 0\end{bmatrix}: a,b,c\in\mathbb{R}\right\}
   			</md>
   		 is a subring of <m>\mathcal{M}_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m>.</p>
	    </exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1in">
		<p>
    The set <m>2\mathbb{Z}_{6} = \{ [0], [2], [4]\}</m> (with addition and
    multiplication defined as in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}</m> ) is a subring of
    <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}</m>.
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1in">
		<p>
    Give an example of a subset of <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}</m> that is <em>not</em> a subring.
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1in">
			<p>For each of the sets above, say the <em>strongest</em> possible thing. E.g., is it a ring? Is it a commutative ring? Is it a ring with identity? Is it an integral domain? Is it a field?</p>
		</exercise>
	</page>
	
	
	<page>
		<paragraphs>
			<title>Ideals</title>
			
		<p>An ideal is a special kind of subring.</p>
		
		<definition>
			<p>
    An <term>ideal</term> <m>I</m> in a ring <m>R</m> is a subring of <m>R</m>
    such that <m>rx\in I</m> and <m>xr\in I</m> for all <m>r\in R</m> and
    <m>x\in I</m>.
			</p>
		</definition>
		
		<p>
    That is, if you multiply an element of <m>R</m> by an element of <m>I</m>
    (in any order), the element of <m>R</m> gets <q>sucked in</q> to <m>I</m>.
    So sometimes we say that ideals have the <q>sucking property</q>.
		</p>
		
		<exercise>
			<introduction>
				<p>Determine whether the following are ideals of <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>. Justify your answer.</p>
			</introduction>
			
			<task workspace="0.5in">
				<p>
    				<m>\{0\}</m>
				</p>
			</task>
			
			<task workspace="0.5in">
				<p>
   					 <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>
				</p>
			</task>
			
			<task workspace="0.5in">
				<p>
    				 <m>\mathbb{E}</m>
				</p>
			</task>
			
			<task workspace="0.5in">
				<p>
    				<m>J = \{3x: x\in \mathbb{Z}\}</m>
				</p>
			</task>
			
			<task workspace="0.5in">
				<p>
    				<m>\mathbb{E}\cap J</m>
				</p>
			</task>
			
			<task workspace="0.5in">
				<p>
    				<m>\mathbb{E}\cup J</m>
				</p>
			</task>
		</exercise>
		</paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	<page>
		<paragraphs>
			<title>Field Extensions</title>
			
		<p>The topic of field extensions is of central importance to much of abstract algebra. Many of the questions pertaining to field extensions have to do with finding solutions to polynomials. E.g.,
<m>
    f(x) = x^{2} -2\in \mathbb{Q}[x]
</m>
, but doesn't have roots in
<m>
    \mathbb{Q}
</m>
(that is no
<m>
    x
</m>
for which
<m>
    f(x)=0
</m>
).</p>

<p>There are two roots for
<m>
    f(x)
</m>
in
<m>
    \mathbb{R}
</m>
:
<m>
    \sqrt{2}
</m>
and
<m>
    -\sqrt{2}
</m>
. But if we use the set
<m>
    \mathbb{Q}\cup \{\sqrt{2},-\sqrt{2}\}
</m>
, then it's not even a ring. So instead we define field extensions, like
<m>
    \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})
</m>
.
</p>

<p>Define
<md>
    \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}) = \{a + b\sqrt{2}: a, b\in \mathbb{Q}\}.
</md>
</p>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<p>
    Show <m>\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})</m> is a subring of <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, and
    therefore is a ring.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<p>
    What do we still need to show that <m>\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})</m> is a field?
    Prove these properties hold.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<p>
    Let <m>F</m> be a field.
    A <term>subfield</term> of <m>F</m> is a subring of <m>F</m> that is also
    a field.
    If <m>F</m> is a subfield of another field <m>E</m> then <m>E</m> is said to
    be a <term>field extension</term> (or simply an <term>extension</term> )
    of <m>F</m>.
    How can <m>\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})</m> be seen as a subfield?
    As a field extension?
    How can <m>\mathbb{C}</m> be seen as a field extension?
</p>
</exercise>

		</paragraphs>
	</page>

<page>
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Direct Sums</title>
		
		<definition>
			 <p>Let <m>R</m>
 and
 <m>
     S
 </m>
 be rings. The
 <term>
     Cartesian product
 </term>
 of
 <m>
     R
 </m>
 and
 <m>
     S
 </m>
 is the set
 <md>
     R\times S = \{ (r,s): r\in R \text{ and }s\in S\}.
 </md>
 The
 <term>
     direct sum
 </term>
 of
 <m>
     R
 </m>
 and
 <m>
     S
 </m>
 , denoted
 <m>
     R\oplus S
 </m>
 is the set
 <m>
     R\times S
 </m>
 with addition and multiplication defined componentwise - that is,
 <md>
     (r_{1}, s_{1}) + (r_{2},s_{2}) = (r_{1} + r_{2}, s_{1} + s_{2}) \text{ and }(r_{1},s_{1})(r_{2},s_{2}) = (r_{1}r_{2},s_{1}s_{2}).
 </md>
 			</p>
		</definition>
		
		<exercise>
			<p>Make addition and multiplication tables for  <m>\mathbb{Z}_2\oplus \mathbb{Z}_3</m>.</p>
		</exercise>
		
